const body = `<h1>2.1b — Object Inspection: Call Methods, Read Fields</h1>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Object Inspector</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you discovered that objects have state (fields) and behavior (methods). Today you'll create multiple objects and inspect them — calling their methods, reading their fields, and seeing how each object is independent.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>ObjectInspector</code> with a main method:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Create two String objects with different values: "Hello" and "Goodbye".</li>
            <li>Call <code>.length()</code>, <code>.toUpperCase()</code>, and <code>.charAt(0)</code> on each. Print all results.</li>
            <li>Create a Rectangle object (from the engine): <code>new Rectangle(0, 0, 50, 30)</code>. Call <code>getWidth()</code> and <code>getHeight()</code>.</li>
            <li>Create a second Rectangle with different values. Call the same methods. Are the results the same?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Observe</h3>
        <p>Two objects of the same class, created with different constructor arguments, return different values from the same method call. What does this tell you about where the data lives?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/objects-and-classes.html">Objects &amp; Classes</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">String Methods</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
